-- title:
--   Valuation rings of a function field in one variable are principal
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x \in F$ be an element such that $F$ is finite-dimensional as a vector space over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` generated by $x$ over the image of $K$ in $F$. Let $O$ be a valuation subring of $F$, that is, a subring of $F$ such that for every $a \in F$ either $a \in O$ or $a^{-1} \in O$. Assume that $O$ contains the image of $K$, in the sense that $\operatorname{algebraMap}_{K,F}(a) \in O$ for every $a \in K$, and that $O$ is not the whole of $F$ (i.e. $O \neq \top$ in the lattice of valuation subrings of $F$). The conclusion is that $O$ is a principal ideal ring: every ideal of $O$ is generated by a single element. Since $O$ is a valuation subring, this is equivalent to saying that $O$ is a discrete valuation ring of rank one, so that the associated valuation on $F^\times$ has value group $\mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   This is the classical fact that every place of an algebraic function field of one variable is discrete, here in a form valid in any characteristic and with no separability or perfectness assumption on the constant field $K$. It underlies the construction of the places and divisors of a curve in this development, and is cited for instance when producing a place from a proper valuation subring and in the study of regular prolongations and of $\operatorname{Pic}^0$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ValuationSubring_isPrincipalIdealRing_of_finiteDimensional_adjoin.lean

